}}

==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Sonic Siren Koneko''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
If [[Koneko (Uber Rare Cat)|Koneko]] is a slot-machine, Metal Maiden Koneko is a game of speed roulette. While Metal Maiden Koneko lacks the small chance of a devastating damage jackpot like her other half, she instead has more reliable, relentless attacks that impede her targets with crowd-control, along with all three Slayer abilities that improve survivability and DPS against some targets.

==='''Normal Form'''===
It is generally ill-advised to use Metal Maiden Koneko over Sonic Siren Koneko. In spite of the faster recharge rate and lower cost, her standing range puts her in constant danger and her attack rate is almost twice as long. She's strictly inferior against [[:Category:Red Enemies|Red]]/[[:Category:Floating Enemies|Floating]] and should only be used if a stage encourages short-ranged, spammable attackers.

Against [[:Category:Zombie Enemies|Zombies]], she finds unique applicability in catching unburrowing Zombies. One of Sonic Siren Koneko's biggest problems is the sheer inconsistency in her abilities, which sometimes let too many Zombies through, and depending on who those enemies are, getting herself badly damaged and/or killed. Since Metal Maiden Koneko's cooldown isn't nearly as long, she's able to be used much more reactively and isn't as punishing if things go south. Regardless, this is still a seldom use-case; Sonic Siren Koneko performs better in most Zombie-centric stages.

==='''Evolved Form'''===
Sonic Siren Koneko is a much stronger backliner, with a standing range of 450. She is able to ward off threats of her respective traits very reliably, continuously [[Knockback|Knocking]] them back, [[Slow|Slowing]] them down, and [[Curse|Cursing]] them, all at a 22% proc rate. She attacks overwhelmingly quickly, and pierces up to 725 via guaranteed [[Explosion]]s, which makes it unlikely for a given enemy to avoid being inflicted by at least one status effect at a time. Knockback and Slow specifically make it difficult for most hyper-aggressive pushers, like [[Teacher Bun Bun]] and [[Bore]], to break through and actually kill her on their own. Even backliners that outrange her aren't safe from crowd-control hell if she's graciously granted a stepping stone to fire Explosions from.

Out of the three abilities, Knockback is the one with the most varied impact on her performance. There are many situations in which it can be good, bad, or both, but it's generally helpful as long as you plan things ahead. Starting with the good: in conjunction with Curse and Slow, Knockback is very good at isolating specific untargeted enemies from their targeted supports. A good example is in [[The Belated Priest]], where Sonic Siren Koneko completely separates [[M. Ost]] from [[Professor A.]] and the [[Zomboe]]s. (Other examples include separating [[Super Metal Hippoe]] from [[Lord Gravey]] in [[Oil Spill Rainbow]], [[Red EnerG]] from the other hyenas in [[Illuminated Trapdoor]], and [[Oldhorn]] from [[Gobble]] in [[Poison on Rice]].) Bursting down a pivotal player from the field is a core component to many strategies, and she ends up making that process a whole lot simpler by sending supports that would normally hinder you on paid leave. Knockback also allows her to simply disregard certain stages entirely, knocking everything behind the enemy base like in [[Ashes Just Ahead]] and [[Candy Downpour]].

However, Knockback + Slow also creates a "[[Mizli (Uber Rare Cat)|Mizli]] Syndrome" of sorts. Since she's so good at Knocking enemies back and Slowing them, she ends up pushing directly into backline supports and/or boss enemies that can't be knocked behind the enemy base, sometimes killing herself in the process. This isn't a very niche occurrence either, and it's especially detrimental on Sonic Siren Koneko, given how long her recharge rate is. You must beware when and where you decide to send her out to ensure you get the most bang for your buck; in a stage like [[Aquatic Avant-garde]], it's better to wait until after [[Nimoy Bore]] spawns to deploy her, as this prevents her from knocking all of the Reds directly into [[Galactic Overseer Nyandam]]'s range. This effect can also be used to your advantage with rushers like [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Awakened Bahamut Cat]] and [[Express Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Bullet Train Cat]], who can bulldoze into the over-extended backline and directly target the problem-enemy before they even have a chance to kill your exposed attackers. Even if worst comes to worst, you shouldn't be afraid to press the advantage at the cost of Sonic Siren Koneko if the opportunity presents itself. Sometimes, the only thing a stage has going for it is a single dangerous backliner like [[Camelle]] or [[Elder Sloth]], and getting rid of them means beating the stage in a roundabout way. If she's gotten her value's worth already and your lineup doesn't suffer much from her loss, it may be okay to simply let her go.

Her effective DPS against the enemy hit by her Single Attack is 7,200 at Lv.30 (11,435 at Lv.50), seeing that Explosion doubles her damage. That said, other enemies will be seeing much lower effective DPS if they arrive in hordes and/or are continuously displaced into her outermost Explosion. Stalling can be good since most stages have limited enemy spawns, but you must have a team that's equipped to handle boss extermination once a stage is exhausted of its biggest threats, especially if Sonic Siren Koneko is unable to deal with cleanup, perhaps due to being naturally outranged by said boss enemy. Think [[Mega-Mooth]] in [[Mega Menace (Old)/On Mighty Wings Lv1~20|On Mighty Wings Lv.11~20]], where you can only get so far without killing the damn thing at some point.

The Slayer abilities are polarized in most situations—outside of [[Sage Slayer]] (which enables her usage against [[Oldstrich]], [[Sage of Mind Soractes]], [[Prof. Owlbrowley]], and [[Doctor K.O.]], all of whom may warrant her crowd-control). The stat buffs won't ever hurt her, but her best [[Behemoth Slayer]] use-cases still come in the form of isolating threats and pummeling their supports, and they can open up new use cases but aren't enough to change certain dynamics determined by other stats like range. That said, at high levels, she's fully capable of abusing the ludicrous 2.5x Behemoth damage multiplier to burst down enemies like [[Crustaceous Scissorex]], [[Elephantidae Papaou]], [[Angelic Beast Rajakong]], and [[Vermilingua Rangmaster]] if they're accompanied by light support that doesn't immediately deny her usage. Be wary—high-damage pierce can delete her in record time, especially if it removes her defense or is not targeted by her Slayer resistance, so pick and choose her battles carefully on this front. After all, [[THE FOLIVOREAN]] and [[Pterowl Hazuku]] are a few leagues above Heavy Metal.

All in all, Sonic Siren Koneko is a good unit, but shouldn't be used recklessly. Plan things out ahead of time, and she'll pay dividends.

==='''Ability Uptimes'''===
''General Ability Activation'': On average, you can expect her to activate at least one CC ability (Slow, Curse, Knockback) about '''~53%''' of the time.

*One CC ability: '''~40%'''
*Two CC abilities: '''~11%'''
*All CC abilities: '''~1.1%'''

''Metal Maiden Koneko/Sonic Siren Koneko's average uptimes (assuming [[Fruit Treasures]])'': The uptime of Slow and Curse are both '''~37%/54%''' individually. You can expect either of the two to be active at any given moment around '''~60%/79%''' of the time, and both to be active simultaneously '''~14%/30%''' of the time. Slow is usually far more useful than Curse in almost every relevant scenario, so keep that in mind when farming RNG.

